Crawl does this (there, it's called "stash tracking"). The purpose of
the feature isn't to give you points for items you've left behind, and
I'm pretty sure it doesn't. What it does do, though, is allow you to 
search all of the items you have ever seen, in-game, and then travel
automatically to these stashes. In some cases you have to manually 
define something as a stash, but by default, Crawl just keeps track of
everything and lets you see where it is. Now, this takes some of the 
burden off of the player (which may not be "the NetHack way"), but you
still have to make the important choices: What to carry, where to leave
what you don't carry, when to go back to stashes, &c.

It even automatically identifies everything for you after you die:

http://crawl.akrasiac.org/rawdata/rax/morgue-rax-20070321-2037.lst

Unfortunately, I think that doing this in NetHack would require some 
major rewriting, and there probably isn't much demand for it. Also, if
only for historical reasons, the idea of NetHack keeping a 100K text
file for every advanced character is a bit terrifying. (That's just the 
list of items; savefiles, levelfiles, &c. are stored elsewhere and are
not viewable by the player.)

(While we're on the subject, Crawl's autotravel would do well in NetHack,
too; "Just bring me to the vibrating square. When I get interrupted, let
me deal with it, and then let me press a key to continue descending." 
Sadly, Crawl's autotravel is stupid about arrow traps and such in the 
same way that NetHack's is.)

Thought this might be of interest to some people here,

